<section id="guide" class="border-t border-white/5 py-16 sm:py-24">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6">
    <h2 class="reveal text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white text-balance text-sheen">What is uptime monitoring, and why does your website need it?</h2>
    <div class="mt-8 space-y-5 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-400 [&_h3]:mt-10 [&_h3]:text-xl [&_h3]:font-semibold [&_h3]:text-white [&_strong]:text-slate-200">
      <p>
        <strong>Website uptime monitoring</strong> means a service checks your site automatically, around the clock, and tells you the moment it stops working. Websites go down for many reasons: an expired SSL certificate, a full server, a broken plugin update, a hosting outage or a forgotten domain renewal. Without monitoring, you usually find out from an angry customer, hours later.
      </p>
      <p>
        UptimeRun is a <strong>free uptime monitor</strong> that checks your website as often as every 30 seconds. If your site is slow to answer, returns an error, or a keyword like “Add to cart” disappears, we re-check to confirm, then send a <strong>website down alert</strong> by email, Telegram, Slack or Discord. You get a second message when it’s back up, with how long the downtime lasted.
      </p>

      <h3>How to monitor your website in 3 steps</h3>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li><strong>Add your website.</strong> Paste the address, like <em>yourshop.com</em>. No code, plugin or server setup.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose how often to check.</strong> Every 5 minutes on Free, every minute on Pro, or every 30 seconds on Business.</li>
        <li><strong>Pick where to get alerts.</strong> Add your email, Telegram, Slack or Discord, and send a test alert to make sure it works.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>What UptimeRun checks</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li><strong>Website (HTTP/HTTPS) uptime:</strong> is the page answering with a success code, and how fast?</li>
        <li><strong>Keyword monitoring:</strong> does an important word still appear on the page? A site can load and still be broken.</li>
        <li><strong>SSL certificate expiry:</strong> we warn you 30, 14, 7 and 1 day before it expires, so visitors never see a security warning.</li>
        <li><strong>Domain expiry:</strong> the same early warnings before your domain name runs out.</li>
        <li><strong>Response time:</strong> a chart of how fast your site answers, so you can spot slowdowns early.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Public status pages for your customers</h3>
      <p>
        A <strong>status page</strong> shows your customers whether your website and services are working. When something breaks, it updates automatically with an incident, and when it’s fixed it shows the outage as resolved. Customers check the page instead of flooding your inbox. Every UptimeRun account includes a free status page with 90 days of uptime history.
      </p>

      <h3>Who uses uptime monitoring?</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li><strong>Online stores</strong> that lose sales every minute the checkout is down.</li>
        <li><strong>Freelancers and agencies</strong> who look after many client websites.</li>
        <li><strong>SaaS products and APIs</strong> that need to know about errors before users report them.</li>
        <li><strong>Bloggers and WordPress sites</strong> where a bad update can break the whole site.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Tips to reduce downtime</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>Monitor your <strong>checkout or login page</strong>, not just the home page, since that’s where problems cost the most.</li>
        <li>Turn on <strong>SSL and domain expiry alerts</strong>. Expired certificates are one of the most common, and most avoidable, outages.</li>
        <li>Send alerts to a <strong>chat app you check often</strong>, like Telegram or Slack, not only email.</li>
        <li>Share your <strong>status page</strong> link in your website footer and help desk.</li>
      </ul>

